# =============================================================================
# Template for /etc/sudoers.d/leiturgia-update  (Cantor host artifact)
#
# This is NOT installed automatically — Castellan/Dennis copies this file to
# /etc/sudoers.d/leiturgia-update on the host (Cantor only) as part of
# provisioning, then:
#
#   sudo install -m 0440 leiturgia-update.sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/leiturgia-update
#   sudo visudo -c
#
# Mode MUST be 0440, and the file MUST be validated with `visudo -c` before
# (and after) installing — sudoers syntax errors can lock out sudo entirely.
#
# Why `pi`: the rule targets the `leiturgia` service's current `User=`, which
# is `pi` (see packaging/leiturgia.service). OQ-1 decided the target end state
# is a dedicated, unprivileged `leiturgia` system account — `pi` is the
# accepted interim per task 5.6, a follow-up migration that has not landed
# yet. When that migration lands, this rule must be retargeted from `pi` to
# `leiturgia` (and `User=` updated in both leiturgia.service and
# leiturgia-update.service to match).
#
# This is the security hinge of the whole feature: it grants the web user
# (running as `pi`) passwordless root privilege to start EXACTLY ONE systemd
# unit — leiturgia-update.service — and nothing else. Do not widen this rule.
# =============================================================================

pi ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl start leiturgia-update.service
